WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:44.855
This is an electrophysiology-level teaching lesson on supraventricular tachycardia, built only from the approved Cardiology Assistant folder and its supplied SVT page screenshots. We will move from the clinical syndrome to the surface ECG, then from the ECG hypothesis to intracardiac proof, acute treatment, chronic treatment, and ablation. The goal is not memorizing isolated drug names. The goal is to recognize the circuit, understand which limb is essential, and choose an intervention that interrupts that limb. This is a textbook-based learning aid, not a substitute for supervised EP training or a patient-specific emergency order set.

00:44.855 --> 01:35.585
Braunwald defines SVT as an umbrella term for tachycardias involving tissue from the His bundle or above. The list includes sinus tachycardia syndromes, focal and multifocal atrial tachycardia, macroreentrant atrial tachycardia including typical flutter, junctional tachycardia, AVNRT, and accessory-pathway-mediated AVRT. Paroxysmal SVT is the clinical syndrome of a rapid regular tachycardia with abrupt onset and abrupt termination. Hurst describes AVNRT as the largest share of PSVT, followed by orthodromic AVRT and then atrial tachycardia. Grossman and Baim give the adult distribution as AVNRT, AVRT, and AT making up the great majority.

01:35.585 --> 02:21.845
Topol describes the core requirements for reentry: two functionally distinct pathways that can form a circuit, unidirectional block in one limb, and slow conduction that allows the blocked limb to recover. A premature beat can therefore initiate a loop instead of producing a single ectopic beat. Park presents AVRT and AVNRT as reciprocating circuits and contrasts them with automatic atrial and junctional ectopic tachycardias. This distinction matters diagnostically: reentrant rhythms are often abrupt and regular and may terminate when AV nodal conduction is interrupted, whereas automatic rhythms can show warm-up or cool-down and may continue through transient AV block.

02:21.845 --> 03:09.110
Before naming a tachycardia, describe it. Is the ventricular rhythm regular or irregular? Is the QRS narrow or wide? Can atrial activity be identified, and is the atrial-to-ventricular relationship one-to-one? For a regular narrow-complex rhythm, measure the RP interval and compare it with the PR interval. For a wide-complex rhythm, keep ventricular tachycardia in the differential while considering aberrant conduction, antidromic AVRT, and other preexcited tachycardias. A surface ECG is a hypothesis generator. The decisive question is whether the atria, AV node, His-Purkinje system, ventricle, or an accessory pathway is required by the circuit.

03:09.110 --> 03:51.735
For AVNRT, orient yourself in the triangle of Koch. Its boundaries are the tendon of Todaro, the coronary sinus ostium, and the septal attachment of the tricuspid valve. The compact AV node is at the apex. Braunwald describes superior fast-pathway inputs and inferior slow-pathway inputs, with additional left-sided inputs that may participate. The clinical circuit can be right sided, left sided, bilateral, or figure-of-eight. This anatomy explains why slow-pathway ablation is delivered in the posterior or inferoseptal region near the coronary sinus ostium, while avoiding the compact node and His bundle.

03:51.735 --> 04:45.805
In sinus rhythm, the fast pathway usually reaches the His bundle first. A premature atrial beat may find the fast pathway refractory and conduct down the slow pathway. The resulting delay allows the fast pathway to recover, conduct retrogradely, and return the impulse to the atrium. If the loop repeats, typical slow-fast AVNRT is established. In the EP laboratory, this physiology appears as a discontinuous AV nodal conduction curve or an AH jump with a small change in atrial coupling interval. Atrial pacing can demonstrate dual physiology, while a carefully timed atrial premature beat can initiate an echo beat and then sustained tachycardia. Dual physiology alone is not the diagnosis; it is a substrate that must be linked to the clinical tachycardia.

04:45.805 --> 05:40.925
Typical AVNRT is a regular narrow-complex tachycardia, often between 120 and 200 beats per minute. Because antegrade conduction is slow and retrograde conduction through the fast pathway is very rapid, atrial and ventricular activation are nearly simultaneous. The P wave is buried in the QRS or just after it. Look for a pseudo R-prime in V1 or pseudo S waves in leads II, III, and aVF. The RP interval is shorter than the PR interval. ST depression and post-termination T-wave inversion can occur during or after AVNRT and, according to Hurst, do not by themselves prove coronary ischemia. The surface pattern is supportive; the response to AV nodal interruption and the EP activation sequence make the mechanism secure.

05:40.925 --> 06:32.600
Atypical AVNRT uses fast-slow or slow-slow activation and usually produces a long-RP tachycardia. The retrograde P wave is more clearly visible and is often negative in the inferior leads. The long-RP differential also includes focal atrial tachycardia, sinus-node reentry, and AVRT using a slowly conducting accessory pathway such as a permanent junctional reciprocating pathway. Do not diagnose from RP length alone. Ask whether atrial activation is part of the circuit, whether the rhythm terminates with AV block, and whether the intracardiac VA sequence is compatible with a nodal circuit. Hurst notes that atypical AVNRT is often inducible with ventricular pacing, unlike typical AVNRT.

06:32.600 --> 07:20.505
AVRT is a large-loop circuit that includes the atrium, the normal AV conduction system, the ventricle, and an accessory pathway. In orthodromic AVRT, the impulse travels antegrade through the AV node and His-Purkinje system, so the QRS is usually narrow; it returns retrogradely through the accessory pathway. The retrograde P wave may deform the ST segment or T wave, often most clearly in V1. A premature ventricular complex can initiate the tachycardia when the pathway and AV node have the right refractory timing. The circuit requires the ventricle and the accessory pathway, which is why ventricular pacing and a His-refractory PVC are powerful EP tests.

07:20.505 --> 08:09.615
A manifest accessory pathway can conduct antegrade during sinus rhythm and produce the WPW pattern: a short PR interval, an initial slurred delta wave, and a widened or morphologically altered QRS. The pathway may be concealed and conduct only retrogradely, leaving no delta wave in sinus rhythm while still supporting orthodromic AVRT. Delta-wave morphology can suggest pathway location, but Braunwald emphasizes that localization is most specific when maximal preexcitation is achieved during EP pacing. A normal sinus ECG therefore does not exclude a concealed accessory pathway, and a delta wave does not by itself tell you which tachycardia is occurring at the bedside.

08:09.615 --> 09:01.565
Antidromic AVRT conducts antegrade over the accessory pathway and retrograde through the AV node or another accessory pathway. Because the ventricle is activated outside the normal His-Purkinje sequence, the rhythm is regular and wide with maximal preexcitation. The P wave may be hidden in the broad QRS. The differential is ventricular tachycardia, SVT with aberrancy, antidromic AVRT, and preexcited atrial or AV nodal tachycardia. In antidromic tachycardia, Braunwald favors drugs active on the accessory pathway when medication is required, because multiple pathways may form either limb. If the patient is unstable or medical therapy fails, synchronized cardioversion is the pathway-independent treatment.

09:01.565 --> 09:55.415
Preexcited atrial fibrillation is irregularly irregular and wide, with variable degrees of preexcitation and sometimes extremely short R-R intervals. Braunwald identifies a shortest preexcited R-R interval below 250 milliseconds as a marker of risk for ventricular fibrillation and sudden death. This is not the ordinary narrow-complex SVT pathway. If the patient is unstable, direct-current cardioversion is indicated in the cited textbook pathway. If stable, Braunwald describes drugs that act on the accessory pathway, including procainamide, ibutilide, or a class 1C agent when appropriate. The text warns against AV-nodal-blocking drugs and amiodarone in preexcited AF because ventricular conduction over the pathway may accelerate.

09:55.415 --> 10:46.940
Focal atrial tachycardia is driven by a discrete atrial focus and often has a P-wave morphology different from sinus rhythm. Braunwald describes warm-up and cool-down as clues to automaticity or triggered activity. Adenosine may terminate the focus, transiently slow it, or simply create AV block that unmasks continuing atrial activity. Macroreentrant atrial tachycardia, including typical flutter, is a circulating wavefront around an anatomic or scar-related obstacle. Vagal stimulation does not terminate flutter, but increased AV block may reveal the saw-tooth atrial activity more clearly. These rhythms are atrial disorders; transient AV block is therefore a diagnostic window rather than proof of AVNRT.

10:46.940 --> 11:44.015
For a regular narrow-complex tachycardia, first inspect the atrial-to-ventricular relationship. Short RP means the retrograde P wave is hidden in or immediately after the QRS; typical AVNRT is the leading mechanism, with orthodromic AVRT also possible. Long RP means the atrial activation is later; consider atypical AVNRT, focal atrial tachycardia, or a slowly conducting accessory pathway. A variable RP or changing P morphology moves you toward atrial tachycardia or another atrial mechanism. Chou shows why the ECG alone can be ambiguous: a P wave deforming the T wave may be AVRT or focal AT. Termination with the final event being a retrograde P wave strongly argues that AV nodal conduction was part of the circuit.

11:44.015 --> 12:33.565
Vagal stimulation and adenosine are not merely rate treatments; they are physiologic experiments. A reentrant rhythm that requires the AV node may terminate when AV nodal conduction is transiently blocked. A focal atrial tachycardia may continue while the ventricles briefly block, exposing the ongoing P waves. Flutter continues, but the ventricular response may slow enough to reveal flutter waves. Hurst describes rapid intravenous adenosine as a 6-milligram bolus followed by 12 milligrams if the first dose is ineffective, with immediate saline flush because the half-life is only seconds. Give it with resuscitation capability and cardioversion available, particularly when an accessory pathway is possible.

12:33.565 --> 13:22.695
For a hemodynamically stable regular narrow-complex SVT, the cited pathway begins with vagal maneuvers, preferably supine with leg elevation. If the rhythm persists, give rapid IV adenosine as described in the source texts. When adenosine fails or the rhythm recurs, IV verapamil or diltiazem, or an IV beta blocker, may be considered in a stable patient when the clinical mechanism and contraindications are appropriate. Synchronized cardioversion is recommended when drug treatment is ineffective or contraindicated and is the immediate branch for hemodynamic instability. The ECG must be reviewed again after conversion because preexcitation may only become obvious in sinus rhythm.

13:22.695 --> 14:14.195
Instability changes the sequence. Shock, ischemic chest discomfort, acute pulmonary edema, altered mental status, or severe hypotension requires synchronized cardioversion rather than a prolonged diagnostic exercise. In a wide-complex rhythm, do not assume SVT simply because the patient has a known accessory pathway. Braunwald's wide-QRS comparison uses initiation sequence, AV relationship, QRS morphology, axis, concordance, initial QRS velocity, and capture or fusion beats. If preexcited AF is suspected, use the separate pathway described earlier and avoid AV-nodal blockade. If the rhythm is uncertain, treat the patient and the rhythm as high risk while obtaining expert EP input.

14:14.195 --> 15:05.435
The ECG clues that favor supraventricular origin include initiation by a premature P wave, tachycardia complexes identical to the resting QRS, a long-short sequence before onset, QRS contours consistent with bundle-branch aberrancy, slowing or termination with vagal maneuvers, a normal axis, and an onset-to-peak QRS interval under 50 milliseconds. Findings favoring ventricular tachycardia include initiation by a premature QRS, AV dissociation or another non-one-to-one relationship, capture or fusion beats, a very broad QRS, left-axis deviation, concordant precordial progression, and a slow initial QRS deflection. These are structured clues, not permission to ignore clinical instability or the need for definitive electrophysiologic assessment.

15:05.435 --> 16:00.040
The highest-yield investigation is documentation of the ECG during symptoms. Obtain a 12-lead ECG in sinus rhythm to look for preexcitation, P-wave abnormalities, and bundle-branch patterns, and obtain the tachycardia tracing whenever possible. Braunwald recommends routine biochemistry and thyroid testing and an echocardiogram to assess ventricular function, atrial size, and significant valve disease. Choose monitoring by symptom frequency: Holter for daily events, longer ambulatory or wearable monitoring for less frequent episodes, and an implantable loop recorder when simpler strategies fail. Kern makes the EP boundary explicit: the surface ECG suggests the mechanism, but detailed EPS is the accurate way to characterize the circuit and define the substrate.

16:00.040 --> 16:51.170
In the EP laboratory, programmed atrial and ventricular pacing, intracardiac electrograms, and activation-sequence analysis define the circuit. For AVNRT, an AH jump, an echo beat, a short VA or HA interval, and a nodal activation pattern support a reentrant AV nodal circuit. For AVRT, ventricular pacing demonstrates retrograde pathway conduction, and a His-refractory premature ventricular complex that advances or terminates the atrium is powerful evidence that an accessory pathway is in the circuit. Entrainment and the post-pacing response help distinguish AVNRT, AVRT, and atrial tachycardia. The principle is simple: change one chamber or one limb and observe which part of the circuit follows.

16:51.170 --> 17:42.575
For AVNRT, the treatment target is the slow-pathway region in the inferoseptal right atrium near the coronary sinus ostium. Junctional beats during radiofrequency delivery can signal an effective site, but very fast junctional rhythm or VA block during junctional rhythm is a warning to stop because antegrade AV block may be impending. Grossman and Baim emphasize that the important endpoint is noninducibility of AVNRT, not necessarily complete elimination of every residual slow-pathway conduction response or single echo beat. The principal material risk is inadvertent AV block. Long sheaths improve catheter stability, and cryoablation is described as an option when reversibility near the AV node is especially valuable.

17:42.575 --> 18:37.455
For AVRT, the electrophysiologist maps the accessory pathway and delivers energy at the pathway insertion or the earliest activation site, with continuous attention to the normal conduction system and coronary anatomy. Braunwald and Hurst describe catheter ablation as the treatment of choice for recurrent symptomatic AVRT or sustained preexcited AF, with contemporary success near 95 percent in the cited chapter. For symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia, the target is the earliest atrial activation of the focus. Macroreentrant atrial tachycardia requires activation and entrainment mapping to identify the critical isthmus. In every substrate, the endpoint is mechanism-specific: eliminate the pathway or focus and demonstrate that the clinical tachycardia is no longer inducible.

18:37.455 --> 19:35.880
Long-term management depends on episode frequency, severity, predictability, occupation, ventricular function, and patient preference. Braunwald allows observation for mild, infrequent episodes that terminate with vagal maneuvers. Recurrent, unpredictable, or severe symptomatic SVT shifts the balance toward EPS and ablation. Slow-pathway ablation is the treatment of choice for AVNRT, accessory-pathway ablation for recurrent AVRT or sustained preexcited AF, and focal ablation for symptomatic focal AT. If ablation is not preferred or is unsuccessful, the cited chapters describe selected AV nodal blockers or antiarrhythmic drugs according to the substrate and the presence or absence of structural or ischemic heart disease. The post-conversion sinus ECG and the patient’s exact mechanism determine what is safe.

19:35.880 --> 20:30.605
Checkpoint one: sudden regular narrow tachycardia, short RP, pseudo R-prime in V1 or pseudo S waves inferiorly. The surface pattern is typical AVNRT; vagal maneuvers and adenosine test an AV-nodal-dependent circuit. Checkpoint two: regular narrow tachycardia with a retrograde P wave deforming the T wave, especially with a concealed pathway. Think orthodromic AVRT, but remember that focal AT can mimic the same P-wave position; a final retrograde P wave at termination favors a circuit requiring the AV node. Checkpoint three: irregularly irregular wide tachycardia with preexcitation and very short R-R intervals. That is the preexcited AF branch, where the usual AV-nodal-blocking algorithm is unsafe in the cited texts.

20:30.605 --> 21:25.990
The EP checklist is: document the rhythm; classify regularity and QRS width; find atrial activity and measure RP; decide whether the AV node, ventricle, or accessory pathway is essential; use vagal maneuvers and adenosine only in the appropriate stable rhythm; separate preexcited AF from ordinary narrow SVT; and refer recurrent symptomatic disease for EPS and mechanism-specific ablation. The visual source pages come from Braunwald PDF pages 1224, 1350 through 1377, and 1384; Hurst pages 2008 through 2014; Chou pages 399 and 403 through 405; Topol-Griffin pages 249 through 261; Grossman and Baim pages 2150 and 2153; Kern page 495; and Park pages 315 and 316. The approved local folder is the complete source boundary for this video.
